      //     {
      //       "country": "Armenia",
      //       "title": "Thailand Joins Armenia, China, Brazil, Philippines, Kenya and More in Redefining Visa-Free Stay Policies for Greater Control Over Tourism, Economic Impact, and Sustainability",
      //       "url": "https://www.travelandtourworld.com/news/article/thailand-joins-armenia-china-brazil-philippines-kenya-and-more-in-redefining-visa-free-stay-policies-for-greater-control-over-tourism-economic-impact-and-sustainability/",
      //       "snippet": "",
      //       "full_content": [
      //         "Thailand is rethinking its visa-free stay policies in alignment with global trends, aiming to have greater control over tourism, boost economic impact, and ensure sustainability. This decision comes as the country, along with nations like Armenia, China, Brazil, the Philippines, and Kenya, seeks to balance tourism growth with the need for stronger immigration regulations and environmental responsibility. With increasing concerns about illegal business activities and the pressure of overtourism, Thailand’s revision of its visa policies is intended to protect local industries, preserve resources, and create a more sustainable tourism model. Thailand is once again taking a hard look at its visa policies for international visitors—a decision that could have far-reaching implications for both the nation’s tourism industry and its national security. Since July 2024, the Thai government has offered visa-free entry for up to 60 days to citizens of 93 countries. This measure, which allowed visitors to extend their stay by an additional 30 days, was implemented to boost tourism, business, and temporary work as the country recovered from the pandemic. However, while the policy helped revive the tourism sector, it also led to an increasing number of illegal activities, prompting the government to reexamine its visa-free entry framework. As global tourism grows and countries reassess their visa rules, Thailand’s decision to review its policies places it among a growing list of nations that are recalibrating visa strategies to balance economic growth, tourism, and national security concerns. Under the leadership of former Prime Minister Srettha Thavisin, Thailand’s visa-free stay for 93 countries was introduced with the goal of stimulating economic recovery and fostering stronger international ties. However, what seemed like a tourism boon also led to an increase in illegal foreign activities. Authorities discovered that several visitors had overstayed their permitted time and were involved in illegal business activities such as operating unregistered travel agencies, unlicensed hotels, and offering unauthorized tour guiding services. These illegal operations not only undermine local businesses but also raise serious concerns about immigration enforcement and national security. As a result, in March 2025, former Prime Minister Paetongtarn Shinawatra ordered a comprehensive study to evaluate the policy’s impact on Thailand’s economy and tourism industry. This study was tasked with finding the “ideal” length of stay for foreign visitors to maximize economic benefits while minimizing negative impacts such as illegal business practices. Unfortunately, political instability in Thailand delayed any concrete decisions. However, under the current leadership of Prime Minister Anutin Charnvirakul, the government has decided to revisit the issue with urgency. On October 22, 2025, the Thai government convened an official meeting to discuss the future of the visa-free entry policy. This session brought together the Department of Consular Affairs, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Ministry of Tourism and Sports, the Ministry of Interior, the National Security Council, and the Immigration Bureau. Together, they discussed the impact of the 60-day visa-free entry rule, and the potential benefits and drawbacks of maintaining or modifying the policy. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs proposed the re-establishment of a Visa Policy Committee that would carefully evaluate the policy’s effectiveness and propose necessary adjustments. According to officials, the urgent focus was on assessing how long visitors should be permitted to stay under the visa exemption policy, particularly for those re-entering the country after leaving.",
      //         "Attakorn Sirilatthayakorn, Thailand’s Minister of Tourism and Sports, has emphasized that the government must strike a balance between promoting tourism and protecting local businesses. He explained that the goal of the meeting was to ensure that Thailand remains an attractive destination for international tourists while curbing illegal activities that negatively affect the local economy. “This is about finding a way to foster tourism, but with better immigration controls to ensure a fair playing field for local businesses,” Sirilatthayakorn said. The government has indicated that a decision on a revised visa-free stay policy is expected before the end of 2025. There is a possibility that the country could revert to the 30-day visa-free policy for all international visitors to reduce illegal activity while still encouraging legitimate tourism. Thailand’s efforts to reassess its visa-free entry rules place it in the company of other countries that are currently revising their visa policies. Governments around the world are recalibrating their visa frameworks to stimulate tourism while addressing issues such as security, illegal activities, and reciprocity. Here’s a look at how other nations are adjusting their visa policies: China is expanding its visa-free transit policy, which was introduced in December 2024. Travelers from 54 countries can now stay up to 10 days (240 hours) without a visa while transiting through 60 ports across 24 provinces. Previously, the visa-free transit period ranged from 72 to 144 hours. The goal of this policy change is to encourage more business and leisure travelers to visit China, especially in high-traffic cities like Beijing, Shanghai, and Guangzhou. By increasing the visa-free transit period, China aims to attract more international travelers and boost its status as a global business hub. In a major shift, Brazil has reintroduced visa requirements for U.S., Canadian, and Australian citizens, effective April 10, 2025. This marks the end of a six-year visa waiver policy for these countries. Brazil has long followed a policy of reciprocity, meaning that its citizens require visas to enter the U.S., Canada, and Australia. As a result, Brazil has decided to reinstate visa requirements for these countries, although U.S. travelers can still apply for an electronic visa. This decision reflects Brazil’s desire to strengthen its control over immigration while ensuring fairness in visa policies. Armenia has introduced a significant change by allowing residents of the United Arab Emirates (UAE) to enter the country visa-free starting July 1, 2025. Previously, only UAE citizens could enter Armenia without a visa. This shift is expected to improve travel between the UAE and Armenia, strengthen bilateral ties, and encourage tourism between the two regions. By expanding the visa-free policy to include residents of the UAE, Armenia is making it easier for people to visit and explore its historic and cultural attractions. In a bid to bolster tourism, the Philippines introduced a visa-free entry policy for Indian nationals starting June 8, 2025. This policy aims to increase tourism from India, a country that has shown significant growth in outbound travel in recent years. Indian arrivals to the Philippines increased by 12% in 2024, and the government hopes this new visa-free policy will further strengthen tourism ties between the two nations. The Philippines is keen on capitalizing on the booming Indian tourism market and becoming a more attractive destination for Indian travelers. Kenya made headlines in May 2025 when it expanded its Electronic Travel Authorization (eTA) system to include more countries, particularly in Africa. Previously, many African nationals were required to obtain a visa to visit Kenya. However, the new eTA exemptions make it easier for citizens of most African countries, along with others previously exempt from visa requirements, to travel to Kenya with minimal red tape. This move is part of Kenya’s broader strategy to increase regional tourism and facilitate smoother travel for international visitors.",
      //         "Uzbekistan is moving toward granting U.S. citizens 30-day visa-free access starting in May 2025. This announcement is part of Uzbekistan’s efforts to increase its appeal to American tourists and showcase its rich cultural heritage. Discussions are also underway to allow citizens of Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan, and Russia to enter Uzbekistan using internal passports, further simplifying travel within the region. Thailand’s decision to reexamine its visa-free entry policy is a reflection of the global trend toward recalibrating visa frameworks. As countries like China, Brazil, Armenia, the Philippines, Kenya, and Uzbekistan make changes to their visa policies, it becomes clear that balancing tourism growth with security concerns is a challenge faced by many nations. For Thailand, the focus is on ensuring that tourism continues to thrive while protecting local businesses from illegal foreign competition. Thailand is revising its visa-free stay policies to enhance control over tourism, address economic concerns, and promote sustainability. This move aligns with global trends as countries like Armenia, China, and Brazil reassess their visa frameworks to balance growth with responsible travel. By revisiting its visa-free entry regulations, Thailand joins a growing group of countries aiming to create more sustainable, secure, and efficient visa systems. Whether the policy is adjusted to revert to a 30-day visa-free stay or remains at 60 days with modifications, the outcome will significantly influence the country’s tourism sector and its international appeal. Travelers should stay informed about the evolving visa rules in Thailand and other destinations to ensure smooth and hassle-free journeys in the future. Advertisement   (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({}); Tags: armenia, china, Thailand, Travel News, visa-free entry I want to receive travel news and trade event updates from Travel And Tour World. I have read Travel And Tour World's  Privacy Notice ."
