      //     {
      //       "country": "Indonesia",
      //       "title": "Indonesia Forces Bali Tourists To Comply With New Passport Rule As Immigration Laws Tighten",
      //       "url": "https://www.travelandtourworld.com/news/article/indonesia-forces-bali-tourists-to-comply-with-new-passport-rule-as-immigration-laws-tighten/",
      //       "snippet": "",
      //       "full_content": [
      //         "Indonesia is now requiring all foreign tourists in Bali to carry their passports at all times as part of a broader effort to tighten immigration controls and curb rising visa violations. This new mandate seeks to improve adherence to immigration rules while simultaneously enhancing the management of tourism on the island— particularly as Bali grapals with visitor overstays and the abuse of tourist visas. The measure is complemented by the island’s major tourism centers receiving a dedicated immigration task force to impose these stringent measures. In a sweeping effort to tighten immigration control, Indonesian authorities have issued a strict mandate requiring all foreign visitors to Bali to carry their passports at all times, signaling a powerful crackdown on non-compliance. The decision comes as part of a broader initiative to combat rising incidents of visa violations and ensure foreign tourists adhere to local immigration laws. As part of this effort, the government has deployed a new Bali Immigration Patrol Task Force, known locally as Satgas, to carry out random spot checks across the island. In a decisive step to strengthen immigration enforcement, Indonesia’s Minister for Immigration, Agus Andrianto, unveiled a formidable task force in early August, made up of 100 skilled officers, poised to intensify control across Bali. The patrols are now stationed in some of Bali’s most frequented tourist areas, including hotspots such as Canggu, Seminyak, Kerobokan, Ubud, Kuta, Sanur, Benoa, Jimbaran, Nusa Dua, and the famous surf beaches of Uluwatu and Bingin. These areas are not only known for their tourist traffic but also for attracting foreign visitors who sometimes overstayed their visas or violated other immigration rules. The introduction of random checks has already sparked a wave of reactions. A number of foreign tourists admitted that they had left their passports behind at their hotels, unaware of the requirement to carry them at all times. However, in these initial instances, no fines were imposed. Instead, the officers issued reminders, emphasizing the importance of always carrying a passport or stay permit—locally known as a KITAS—when traveling around the island. The decision to increase patrols and conduct checks on foreign visitors stems from concerns over the rising number of visa violations reported earlier this year. According to Indonesia’s Immigration Law No. 6 of 2011, specifically Article 71, foreign nationals (WNA) are legally required to present their immigration documents to officials when requested. The Bali government, in its bid to enforce these regulations, is taking a more proactive approach, ensuring that all visitors comply with local laws. While some tourists have expressed their confusion over the requirement, the immigration authorities have been keen to emphasize the importance of compliance with the nation’s legal framework. This step also aligns with Indonesia’s broader commitment to addressing illegal immigration and ensuring that foreign visitors respect the country’s regulations. The deployment of the new immigration task force is seen as a significant development in Bali’s ongoing efforts to manage foreign tourism effectively. The 100-officer Satgas team is tasked with conducting these spot checks and monitoring foreign nationals across Bali. Their presence is particularly visible in high-traffic tourist spots, where foreign visitors often congregate. In addition to the passport checks, the officers are also responsible for ensuring that tourists do not overstay their visas, a common issue in the island’s bustling tourist economy.",
      //         "These checks are also aimed at preventing misuse of long-term visas, with many foreign residents in Bali being suspected of using tourist visas for extended stays. The immigration authorities are keen to address this issue and have even indicated that further action may be taken in the future to target areas such as co-working spaces and yoga studios, where the misuse of long-term tourist visas is thought to be common. The intensified immigration checks come at a time when Bali has also introduced a new foreign tourist levy. The fee, amounting to IDR 150,000 (approximately £7.40), is collected by the Bali Tourism Office and applies to foreign visitors entering the island. While the tax has been in place for a while, there are discussions about involving immigration officers in verifying the payment of this levy in future. This move would make the process more streamlined, ensuring that all foreign visitors have paid the necessary tax before entering the island. The introduction of the tourist levy is a strategic move by Bali to take control of its tourism impact, ensuring the preservation of its delicate environment and resources while managing the growing influx of visitors. Some have welcomed this move, noting that the funds could be directed towards improving infrastructure and supporting sustainable tourism initiatives. However, others have expressed concerns that it may deter travelers, particularly those on a budget, from visiting the island. The government’s decision to ramp up immigration checks has sparked a mix of reactions. While some Indonesian citizens have voiced their support for the crackdown, arguing that it will help control visa misuse, others have cautioned that the policy could have unintended consequences. Many fear that the more aggressive approach could discourage visitors from coming to Bali, particularly those seeking a relaxed and hassle-free holiday experience. Some locals, particularly those working in the tourism and hospitality industries, have suggested that the patrols should be extended to places such as co-working spaces and yoga studios, where long-term foreign residents may be taking advantage of tourist visas to live and work on the island. However, such suggestions have raised concerns about the potential for alienating legitimate tourists who come to Bali for short stays and leisure. Bali has long been a popular destination for international tourists, thanks to its stunning landscapes, vibrant culture, and diverse recreational opportunities. However, with increasing reports of visa violations, the authorities have been under pressure to ensure that the island remains a safe and welcoming destination for visitors. While the crackdown on visa violations is likely to address some of the challenges faced by immigration authorities, it could also result in negative publicity, especially if tourists feel that they are being unfairly targeted. On the other hand, some see the crackdown as a necessary step to protect Bali’s long-term tourism industry. By ensuring that foreign visitors comply with the rules, the government hopes to create a more sustainable and organized tourism environment that benefits both tourists and local residents.",
      //         "Indonesia is enforcing a new rule requiring Bali tourists to carry their passports at all times to strengthen immigration control and tackle rising visa violations. This move is part of a broader effort to ensure compliance with local laws and enhance tourism management on the island. Bali’s new policy requiring foreign visitors to carry their passports at all times is part of a broader effort by Indonesian immigration authorities to curb visa violations and improve tourism management. While the move has sparked mixed reactions, it underscores the government’s commitment to ensuring that foreign visitors comply with the country’s immigration laws. As Bali continues to balance tourism growth with sustainability, this new policy may serve as a model for other regions in Indonesia facing similar challenges. However, careful consideration will be needed to ensure that the initiative does not deter legitimate travelers from enjoying the island’s many attractions. Advertisement   (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({}); Tags: bali tourism, Foreign visitors, Indonesia immigration laws, passport requirement, Travel News I want to receive travel news and trade event updates from Travel And Tour World. I have read Travel And Tour World's  Privacy Notice ."
