      //       "country": "Iran",
      //       "title": "Refugee Council's letter to Immigration and Foreign Affairs ministers regarding refugees and Australian citizens trying to depart Iran and Israel",
      //       "url": "https://www.refugeecouncil.org.au/letter-refugees-australian-citizens-depart-iran-israel/",
      //       "snippet": "",
      //       "full_content": [
      //         "The Hon. Tony Burke MP Minister for Home Affairs Minister for Immigration and Citizenship Senator the Hon Penny Wong MP Minister for Foreign Affairs We write to you to enquire about the Australian Government’s plans to support refugees granted Australian Humanitarian XB sub-class visas unable to depart Iran as a result of the recent and substantial escalation of conflict. At the end of 2024, Iran was hosting over 3.89 million refugees and displaced people, primarily people from Afghanistan. The current war escalation has meant that Iranian citizens and refugees from the region residing in Iran all face further displacement and uncertainty. Refugee-led organisations in Australia and in the region have disclosed troubling developments for refugees in Iran over the past week, including arrests, further deportations and forced participation in armed conflict. Iran was the top residence country for Humanitarian visa lodgements in 2023-24, with over 30% of all Humanitarian visas applications made from Iran, equal to over 25,480 applications, overwhelmingly Afghans. Iran features regularly in the top three countries of Australian Humanitarian visas granted, with over 1,600 granted in 2023-24. We understand that the Government has been working to find solutions for the thousands of Australian citizens and permanent residents who have registered to leave Iran and Israel and are unable to travel due to the flight restrictions. The Refugee Council of Australia (RCOA) and our members are seeking the inclusion of refugees holding Humanitarian XB sub-class visas in evacuation plans. Based on recent trends, our understanding is that there would be several hundred refugees residing in Iran who have been granted a Humanitarian XB sub- class visa who have not yet been able to travel to Australia. Any clarity that the Departments of Home Affairs or Foreign Affairs and Trade can provide in relation to changed country conditions for Iran would also be helpful, as there are concerns that people engaged in voluntary return processes may lose support if they are reluctant about continuing their departure arraignments under the current circumstances. Similarly, for people undergoing refugee protection determinations or those who have been given negative decisions require options for further consideration in light of the escalating conflict.",
      //         "The Refugee Council continues its work with Professor Jane McAdam at the Kaldor Centre, SSI, Amnesty International and several refugee and diaspora communities on developing clear options for an Australian framework and predictable protocol in relation to emergent humanitarian crises. This emergency is the sixth major global humanitarian crisis since 2021, in addition to the longstanding conflicts and protracted refugee situations in our region and across the world. There is an urgent need for clarity from the Australian Government on how it responds to global humanitarian crises. We would be happy to meet to further discuss the options available to the Government as it develops its response. There are options available that can be implemented quickly and efficiently and would ensure certainty for families, communities, services, and all three levels of government. Any further information and answers to our queries that you can provide would be helpful. I am available anytime on ceo@refugeecouncil.org.au to discuss these matters. Your sincerely, Paul Power Chief Executive Officer Refugee Council of Australia 250620 Letter to Ministers Iran and Israel Size : 125.5 kB Format : PDF This brief explains why we need to break the artificial ink between the onshore (within Australia) and offshore (overseas resettlement) components of the Humanitarian Program to address the backlog of visas for people... 26 November 2025 The Refugee Council of Australia (RCOA) has called for the Australian Government to immediately suspend its reception arrangement with Nauru, amid reports of serious corruption and indications that the...",
      //         "26 November 2025 One year after the Australian Human Rights Commission launched the country’s first national roadmap to eliminate racism, 54 civil society organisations are calling on the Australian government to... 26 November 2025 One year ago, the Australian Human Rights Commission released the first national roadmap to eliminate racism in Australia: The National Anti-Racism Framework. Developed after extensive consultation with... Suite 4A6, 410 Elizabeth Street Surry Hills 2010 NSW Australia Phone: +61 (02) 9211 9333 Fax: +61 (02) 9211 9288 All election related material is authorised by P. Power, Refugee Council of Australia, Sydney. We pay our respects to the Traditional Custodians of the lands we now call home, their elders past and present. As we work towards a more inclusive future with fair and humane policies for those seeking sanctuary, we remain committed to working alongside First Nations people for the achievement of a fully reconciled Australia. The election scorecard should have downloaded (even if you do not sign up)."
